# Defanged reconstruction of a real malicious package, for scanner tests.
# Never extract and run. See internal/e2e/livescan/corpus.go.
id: telnyx-4.87.2
title: "telnyx 4.87.2 (PyPI): import-time loader takes its second stage out of the audio frames of a WAV file"
campaign: teampcp-trivy-2026
date: "2026-03-27"
kind: wheel
ecosystem: pip
name: telnyx
version: "4.87.2"
benignVersion: "4.87.0"
summary: >-
  On 2026-03-27 the TeamPCP actor published telnyx 4.87.1 (03:51 UTC) and
  4.87.2 (04:07 UTC per Endor Labs) to PyPI, the official Python SDK of the
  Telnyx telephony platform, using a stolen publishing token: neither release
  has a commit, tag or GitHub release, the project used no Trusted Publisher,
  and how the token leaked is not established (Endor Labs suspects the litellm
  compromise three days earlier). PyPI quarantined both by 10:13 UTC (Telnyx,
  GHSA-955r-262c-33jc; Phoenix Security says about 07:00, reports differ). Only
  telnyx/_client.py changed, and nothing runs at install time: importing
  telnyx calls setup() (Windows) and FetchAudio() (Linux and macOS). 4.87.1
  spelled the first call Setup(), a NameError that stopped it, so 4.87.2 is
  the complete version. Each path downloads a "WAV" from a raw IP over plain
  HTTP on port 8080 (ringtone.wav or hangup.wav, Mozilla/5.0 User-Agent), reads
  its frames with the wave module, base64-decodes them and XORs everything
  after the first 8 bytes with those 8 bytes. On Linux and macOS the result, a
  332-line credential harvester, is piped to a fresh python process; its
  output is AES-256-CBC encrypted, the key wrapped with the same RSA-4096 key
  as in litellm, and POSTed as tpcp.tar.gz with an X-Filename header. Endor
  Labs lists SSH keys, cloud and Kubernetes credentials, .env files and
  secrets-manager contents as the loot, Socket calls it only a data harvester,
  and reports differ on Linux persistence (Endor Labs and Phoenix Security: an
  audiomon systemd user service; Socket: none). On Windows the decoded bytes
  are a PE dropped as msbuild.exe in the Startup folder, with a hidden
  msbuild.exe.lock allowing one download per 12 hours. Endor Labs found it by
  diffing the wheels against the v4.87.0 tag and reported it in GitHub issue
  #235.
techniques: [stolen-publishing-token, import-time-execution, audio-steganography, base64-xor-decoding, fileless-execution, credential-theft, rsa-aes-exfiltration, startup-folder-persistence]
reconstruction: >-
  telnyx/_client.py follows the descriptions of Socket, SafeDep, StepSecurity,
  Endor Labs and Phoenix Security: extra standard-library imports at the top
  (os twice), a _d() base64 string helper, the _p blob between __all__ and the
  class, setup() and FetchAudio() after the class, and the two bare calls at
  the bottom in that order. setup() returns unless on Windows and passes
  APPDATA, the Startup path, msbuild.exe and the URL through _d(); it keeps the
  43200 second lock (attrib +h), the msbuild.exe.tmp staging file and
  creationflags 0x08000000. FetchAudio() returns on Windows and otherwise
  starts _p in a detached interpreter (start_new_session) with its output
  discarded. The frame extraction is the one the analyses quote (readframes,
  b64decode, an 8-byte key). _p is not the original 4,428-character blob but a
  short stage written from the descriptions: it fetches ringtone.wav, decodes
  it, pipes the result to "python -", then encrypts and POSTs what comes back,
  with the truncated throwaway RSA key of the litellm sample. The harvester and
  the PE were never in the wheel (they sit in the WAV on the C2) and are not
  reproduced. The C2 is the documentation address 203.0.113.7:8080, a raw IP
  over HTTP as in the original. 4.87.1's Setup() typo is not modelled, and as
  in the real wheels only _client.py differs from 4.87.0 (_version.py still
  says 4.87.0). The rest of the SDK is a tiny stand-in for the generated
  client (the real _client.py has about 7,800 lines). The second indicator is
  the base64 of "msbuild.exe", which the loader decodes at run time.
references:
  - https://telnyx.com/resources/telnyx-python-sdk-supply-chain-security-notice-march-2026
  - https://osv.dev/vulnerability/GHSA-955r-262c-33jc
  - https://socket.dev/blog/telnyx-python-sdk-compromised
  - https://endorlabs.com/learn/teampcp-strikes-again-telnyx-compromised-three-days-after-litellm
  - https://safedep.io/malicious-telnyx-pypi-compromise/
  - https://www.stepsecurity.io/blog/teampcp-plants-wav-steganography-credential-stealer-in-telnyx-pypi-package
  - https://www.trendmicro.com/en/research/26/c/teampcp-telnyx-attack-marks-a-shift-in-tactics.html
  - https://phoenix.security/teampcp-telnyx-pypi-supply-chain-wav-steganography-windows-persistence/
legitimateHosts:
  - api.telnyx.com
indicators:
  - FetchAudio
  - "bXNidWlsZC5leGU="
